   "text": "**Cold drops tap the glass,**\n**Gold leaves drift in muddy streams,**\n**Earth breathes out its sigh.**\n\n### Imagery Explanation\n\nThis haiku relies on sensory details to evoke the specific mood of autumn:\n\n1.  **Auditory and Tactile (\"Cold drops tap the glass\")**: The opening line establishes the immediate physical sensation of the rain. The word \"cold\" sets the temperature, while \"tap\" suggests a gentle, persistent rhythm rather than a violent storm, creating a sense of quiet introspection.\n2.  **Visual (\"Gold leaves drift in muddy streams\")**: This line provides the classic visual marker of autumn\u2014falling leaves. The contrast between the bright \"gold\" leaves and the dull \"muddy\" streams highlights the transition from the vibrancy of summer to the decay and wetness of late autumn. The word \"drift\" implies a slow, inevitable movement, mirroring the passage of time.\n3.  **",
